[UTCTime "09:00:44"]

1. g4 Rxa2 { The mistakes begin. This is the first. Black sees that he can take a Pawn without any danger, and does not stop to think whether there is anything better. 1...Rf2+ was the right move. If then 2 Kg3, Rxa2. If instead White played 2 Ke4, then ...Re5+ followed by Rxa2. } 2. Rd1 Ra4+ { Mistake number two, and this time such a serious one as to almost lose the game. The proper move was to play 2...f5 in order to break up White's Pawns and at the same time make room for the Black King, which is actually in danger, as will soon be seen. } 3. Rd4 Raa5 { Mistake number three and this time fatal. His best move was 3...Rga5. After the text move there is no defense. Black's game is lost. This shows that even an apparently simple ending has to be played with care. From a practically won position Black finds himself with a lost game, and it has only taken three moves. } 4. Rdd8 Rg7 { If: } (4... f5 5. Rh8+ Kg6 6. Rcg8+ Kf6 7. Rxh6+ Rg6 8. g5+ Ke7 9. Rhxg6 fxg6 10. Rg7+ Ke8 11. Rxg6 { and wins easily. }) 5. h4 h5 6. Rh8+ { Black resigns.
The reason is evident. If } 6... Kg6 7. gxh5+ Rxh5 8. Rxh5 Kxh5 9. Rh8+ Kg6 10. h5# *


[Event "Chess Fundamentals, by J.R. Capablanca (Part 2 of 3): Ex.59: Endings with one Rook and Pawns"]
[Date "2021.03.05"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Chess Fundamentals, by J.R. Capablanca (Part 2 of 3)"]
[ChapterName "Ex.59: Endings with one Rook and Pawns"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/CaZ01HLL/mtxdAo5R"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/frankv53"]
[FEN "8/7R/8/2k2P2/1p6/3r4/5PKP/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2021.03.05"]
[UTCTime "09:00:44"]

1. f6 Rd6! { Now White has two continuations, either (a) f7, or (b) Rc7+.
We have therefore: } 2. f7 (2. f7 Rd8! (2... Rg6+ 3. Kf3 Rf6+ 4. Ke3 Re6+ { If 4...b3; Rh5+ wins, because if the King goes back , then Rh6, and if the King goes up, then Rh4+, followed by Rf4 wins. } 5. Kd3 Rf6 { If 5...Rd6+; 5 Ke4 wins. } 6. Rh5+ Kd6 7. Rh6 { wins. }) (2... Rf6 3. Rg7! Kc4 { If 3...b3; 4 Rg3, and White will either capture the Pawn or go to f3, and come out with a winning ending. } 4. h4 b3 5. Rg4+ Kd3 6. Rg3+ { and White will either capture the Pawn or play Rf3, according to the circumstances, and come out with a winning ending.
OR-- }) 3. Rh5+ Kc4 { and White will finally have to sacrifice the Rook for Black's Pawn. }) (2. Rc7+ Kd4! (2... Kb6 3. f7 Rg6+ 4. Kf1 Rf6 5. Re7! Kc5 { White threatened to check with Rook at e6. } 6. Ke2 b3 { Best. If 4...Kc4; both 5 h4 and 5 Ke3 will win; the last-named move particularly would win with ease. } 7. Re3 b2 8. Rb3 Rxf7 9. Rxb2 Rh7 10. Rd2 Rxh2 11. Ke3 { This position we have arrived at is won by White, because there are two files between the opposing King and the Pawn from which the King is cut off by the Rook, and besides, the Pawn can advance to the fourth rank before the opponent's Rook can begin to check on the file. This last condition is very important, because if, instead of the position on the diagram, the Black Rook were at h8, and Black had the move, he could draw by preventing the advance of the Pawn, either through constant checks or by playing ...Rf8 at the proper time.
Now that we have explained the reasons why this position is won, we leave it to the student to work out the correct solution.
The fact that out of one apparently simple ending we have been able to work out several most unusual and difficult endings should be sufficient to impress upon the student's mind the necessity of becoming well acquainted with all kinds of endings, and especially with endings of Rook and Pawns. }) 3. f7 Rg6+! { a very important move, as against 3...Rf6, 4 Re7 wins. } 4. Kf1 Rf6 5. Rb7 Kc3 { and White will finally have to sacrifice the Rook for the Pawn, or draw by perpetual check. }) 2... Rd8! (2... Rg6+ 3. Kf3 Rf6+ 4. Ke3 Re6+ { If 4...b3; Rh5+ wins, because if the King goes back , then Rh6, and if the King goes up, then Rh4+, followed by Rf4 wins. } 5. Kd3 Rf6 { If 5...Rd6+; 5 Ke4 wins. } 6. Rh5+ Kd6 7. Rh6 { wins. }) (2... Rf6 3. Rg7! Kc4 { If 3...b3; 4 Rg3, and White will either capture the Pawn or go to f3, and come out with a winning ending. } 4. h4 b3 5. Rg4+ Kd3 6. Rg3+ { and White will either capture the Pawn or play Rf3, according to the circumstances, and come out with a winning ending.
OR-- }) 3. Rh5+ Kc4 { and White will finally have to sacrifice the Rook for Black's Pawn. } *


[Event "Chess Fundamentals, by J.R. Capablanca (Part 2 of 3): Ex.60: A Difficult Ending: Two Rooks and Pawns"]
[Date "2021.03.05"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Chess Fundamentals, by J.R. Capablanca (Part 2 of 3)"]
[ChapterName "Ex.60: A Difficult Ending: Two Rooks and Pawns"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/CaZ01HLL/yErDhIHG"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/frankv53"]
[FEN "r4rk1/p5pp/2p5/3p1p2/2p5/2P1PP2/PPK2P1P/3R1R2 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2021.03.05"]
[UTCTime "09:00:44"]

1... Rae8 { The first move is already wrong. There is nothing to gain by this move. Black should play ...a5; to be followed by ...a4; unless White plays b3. That would fix the Queen's side. After that he could decide what demonstration he could make with his Rooks to keep the opponent's Rooks at bay. } 2. Rd4 { This move not only prevents ...f4 which Black intended, but threatens b3, followed, after ...cxb3+, by the attack with one or both Rooks against Black's Q R P. } 2... Rf6 { probably with the idea of a demonstration on the King's side by ...Rg6 and ...Rg2. } 3. b3 cxb3+ 4. axb3 Kf7 5. Kd3 { Ra1 should have been played now, in order to force Black to defend with ...Re7. White, however, does not want to disclose his plan at once, and thus awaken Black to the danger of his position, hence this move, which seems to aim at the disruption of Black's Queen's side Pawns. } 5... Re7 6. Ra1 Ke6 { This is a mistake. Black is unaware of the danger of his position. He should have played ...g5; threatening ...Rh6, and, by making this demonstration against White's h-pawn, stop the attack against his Queen's side Pawns, which will now develop. } 7. Ra6 Rc7 { He could not play 7...Kd6, because 8 c4 would win at least a Pawn. This in itself condemns his last move ...Ke6, which has done nothing but make his situation practically hopeless. } 8. Rda4 g5 { Now forced, but it is a little too late. He could not play 8...Rff7, because 9 f4 would have left his game completely paralyzed. Black now finally awakens to the danger, and tries to save the day by the counter-demonstration on the King's side, which he should have started before. Of course, White cannot play Rxa7, because of ...Rxa7, followed by ...Rh6, recovering the Pawn with advantage. } 9. h4! g4 { Black is now in a very disagreeable position. If he played 9...gxh4; 10 Rxh4 would leave him in a very awkward situation, as he could not go back with the King, nor could he do much with either Rook. He practically would have to play 10...h6, when White would answer 11 b4, threatening to win a Pawn by b5, or, if that were not enough, he might play Kd4, to be followed finally by the entry of the King at c5 or e5. } 10. Ke2 gxf3+ { Again he cannot play ...h5, because f4 would leave him paralyzed. The advance of his h-pawn would make White's h-pawn safe, and consequently his King's Rook would have to retire to f7 to defend the a-pawn. That would make it impossible for his King to go to d7, because of the a-pawn, nor could he advance a single one of his Pawns. On the other hand, White would play b4, threatening to win a Pawn by b5, or he might first play Kd4, and then at the proper time b5, if there was nothing better. Black meanwhile could really do nothing but mark time with one of his Rooks. Compare this bottling-up system with the ending in Example 57, and it will be seen that it is very similar. } 11. Kxf3 Rff7 12. Ke2 { Probably wrong. 12 b4 at once was the right move. The text move gives Black good chances of drawing. } 12... Kd6 13. b4 Rb7 { This could never have happened had White played 12 b4, as he could have followed it up by b5 after Black's ...Kd6. } 14. h5 { Not good. 14 f4 offered the best chances of winning by force. If then 14...Rg7; 15 h5, Rg2+; 16 d3, Rh2; 17 Rxa7, Rxa7; 18 Rxa7, Rxh5; 19 Ra6, with winning chances. } 14... h6 { Black misses his last chance. 14...f4 would draw. If then 15 exf4, Rbe7+!; 16 Kf1, Rxf4; 17 Rxa7, Re3 ! } 15. f4 Rg7 16. Kd3 Rge7 17. Ra1 Rg7 18. Kd4 Rg2 19. R1a2 Rbg7 { 19...Rgg7 would have offered greater resistance, but the position is lost in any case. (I leave the student to work this out.) } 20. Kd3! Rxa2 21. Rxa2 Re7 { Nothing would avail. If 21...Rg1; 22 Ra6!, Rd1+; 23 Kc2, Rh1; 24 b5, Rxh5; 25 Rxc6+, Kd7; 26 Ra6, and White will win easily. } 22. Rg2 Re6 23. Rg7 Re7 24. Rg8 c5 { Black is desperate. He sees he can no longer defend his Pawns. } 25. Rg6+ Re6 26. bxc5+ Kd7 27. Rg7+ Kc6 28. Rxa7 Kxc5 29. Rf7 { Black resigns. } *


